• I’ll inspect the existing specifications, scanner proofs, and repository state, then add the trivia judgment, total scanner, proofs, and conformance cases before running the required checks.

• The existing comment judgment gives exactly the needed comment boundary. I’ll define `Trivia` recursively with an explicit stopping constructor, and use input length as the scanner’s termination measure so comment consumption is proved to make progress.

• I’m adding only the settled source-text rule: four ASCII whitespace scalars and maximal runs of whitespace or `//` comments. The stopping constructor explicitly excludes every other opener or scalar.

• The specification elaborates cleanly. Next I’ll add the Boolean whitespace test, prove that comments shrink the input, and use that fact for `skipTrivia`’s termination and its soundness/completeness proofs.

• The scanner and proofs are in place; I’m elaborating them now to check the generated recursion cases and termination obligations before adding the conformance matrix.

• Elaboration exposed only proof-script issues around the dependent `Option` match; the judgment and termination measure themselves were accepted. I’ll simplify that match by scanning the `//` branch directly through the existing `scanLine`, which also makes the conformance reductions more direct.

• The direct branch is not necessary: Lean’s explicit `skipTrivia.eq_def` equation and rewriting the match scrutinee address the failures without changing the scanner. I’ll keep the `lineComment`-based implementation and tighten those proof steps.

• Soundness and the stopping half of completeness now elaborate. The remaining comment case needs the same explicit scrutinee rewrite as soundness, rather than unfolding `lineComment` inside the dependent match.

• Both correspondence proofs and uniqueness now elaborate. I’ll add the required accepted and rejected examples, including explicit checks that non-ASCII spaces, BOM, `/*`, `#!`, and a lone slash consume no trivia.

• The proofs pass, but kernel `decide` cannot reduce well-founded recursion. I’ll replace the implementation with a structurally recursive, length-fueled helper and prove the helper correct whenever its fuel bounds the input length; `skipTrivia` will supply exactly that bound. This preserves totality and makes every conformance case reducible by the kernel.

• The conformance examples and changelog are written, but their kernel reduction requires this implementation change. I’m returning to the scanner proof step until both the correspondence theorems and the examples pass.

• The fueled scanner now reduces and only two completeness goals remain: simplification split pair equality into component equalities. I’ll discharge those directly from the induction hypothesis’s pair equality.

• The structural implementation, both correspondence proofs, uniqueness, and all `decide` conformance cases now elaborate without warnings. I’ll run the full build, regenerate the axiom inventory, and inspect its diff to ensure existing entries are unchanged.

• The golden diff contains exactly one new entry, `ConcreteSpec.Surface0.Trivia.brecOn`, with no axioms; every existing line is unchanged. I’ll now run the required axiom check and build the lexer module itself as well as the default target.

• All required checks pass. I’m reviewing the final diff for scope and rule compliance, then I’ll commit only the four requested files on `wip/slice0-lexer-ws` without pushing.

• The final diff stays within the requested scope: no token or diagnostic rules, no prohibited proof features, and no documentation decisions were touched. The only generated golden addition is axiom-free. I’ll stage these four files and create the requested local commit.

